engdu, People's Republic of China\nL52: \nL53: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL54: \nL55: 【10†Search for more papers by this author】\nL56: \nL57: 【11†Jinyu Xiong】,\nL58: Jinyu Xiong\nL59: \nL60: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL61: \nL62: 【11†Search for more papers by this author】\nL63: \nL64: 【12†Qing He】,\nL65: \nL66: Qing He\nL67: \nL68: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL69: \nL70: 【12†Search for more papers by this author】\nL71: \nL72: 【7†Xinxin Ouyang】,\nL73: \nL74: Corresponding Author\nL75: \nL76: Xinxin Ouyang\nL77: \nL78:   * 【13†[email protected]】\nL79: Electronic Engineering College, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL80: \nL81: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL82: \nL83: 【7†Search for more papers by this author】\nL84: \nL85: 【9†Qun Wan】,\nL86: \nL87: Qun Wan\nL88: \nL89: Electronic Engineering College, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL90: \nL91: 【9†Search for more papers by this author】\nL92: \nL93: 【10†Jingmin Cao】,\nL94: \nL95: Jingmin Cao\nL96: Electronic Engineering College, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL97: \nL98: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL99: \nL100: 【10†Search for more papers by this author】\nL101: \nL102: 【11†Jinyu Xiong】,\nL103: \nL104: Jinyu Xiong\nL105: \nL106: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL107: \nL108: 【11†Search for more papers by this author】\nL109: \nL110: 【12†Qing He】,\nL111: \nL112: Qing He\nL113: National Key Laboratory of Science and Technology on Blind Signal Processing, Chengdu, People's Republic of China\nL114: \nL115: 【12†Search for more papers by this author】\nL116: \nL117: First published: 01 February 2017\nL118: \nL119: 【14†https://doi.org/10.1049/iet-spr.2016.0299Digital Object Identifier (DOI)†doi.org】\nL120: \nL121: view metrics\nL122: \nL123: Sections\nL124: \nL125: CITE\nL126: \nL127: 【15†Tools】\nL128: \nL129: Share full-text access\nL130: \nL131: Please review our 【16†Terms and Conditions of Use†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】 and check box below to share full-text version of article.\nL132: [Input]I have read and accept the Wiley Online Library Terms and Conditions of Use\nL133: \nL134: * * *\nL135: \nL136: Shareable Link\nL137: \nL138: Use the link below to share a full-text version of this article with your friends and colleagues. 【17†Learn more.†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】\nL139: \nL140: [Button: Copy URL]\nL141: \nL142: Share a link\nL143: \nL144: Share on\nL145: ## Abstract\nL146: The classic two-step approach for time difference of arrival (TDOA) geolocation is suboptimal since the TDOA measurements have not followed the constraint that all measurements should be consistent for a geolocation of a single emitter. In this study, the direct TDOA geolocation approach is proposed for frequency-hopping (FH) emitters.\nL147: It makes use of the sparsity of the FH signals in frequency domain, and constructs a cross correlation function (CCF) matrix in frequency domain, then the location estimate is obtained by searching the maximum eigenvalue of the CCF matrix in a two dimensional grid. The Cramer–Rao lower bound has been derived. The resolution for a single FH signal geolocation is also analysed. Further, an extension of the new method for multiple FH emitters direct TDOA geolocation has been presented.\nL148: The performance comparison between the direct approach and the conventional two-step method has been made by simulations. The results demonstrated that the proposed method outperforms the conventional two-step method. The simulations also demonstrated the effectiveness of the new method in locating multiple FH emitters.\nL149: ## 1 Introduction\nL150: Due to its outstanding performance for resistance of narrowband interference and anti-interception, frequency-hopping spread spectrum has found wide applications in military and commercial wireless communications. Recently, the localisation of FH signal sources has attracted much interest [【18†1】, 【19†2】].\nL151: Generally, the hop band of FH communication system is becoming wider and wider, and the frequency carriers are becoming more and more, which brings a big challenge to the passive localisation of FH emitters.\nL152: Passive localisation of multiple emitters using multi-sensors has found wide applications in wireless communications, sonar, radio astronomy, radar and seismology [【20†3】-【21†10】]. Passive localisation of signal emitters can be achieved by various methods and algorithms.\nL153: The methods and algorithms usually include two steps, first to measure the properties depending on emitter location of the signal, such as angle of arrival (AOA), time difference of arrival (TDOA), frequency difference of arrival (FDOA), or the receive signal strength (RSS), and then use them to estimate the emitter location.\nL154: The AOA based location system or direction-finding system needs two or more stations equipped with antenna array to intercept the emitters signal, and then each station gives an AOA estimate independently to produce an estimate of the emitter position [【22†6】, 【23†11】]. These systems are usually complicated, as the array elements of antenna needs to be synchronised exactly, and the location estimate is not accurate enough for wideband signals.\nL155: When there are relative velocities between sensors and emitters, FDOA can be used for location. The accuracy of FDOA measurements is proportional to the relative velocity, thus the FDOA measurements are usually used in the satellite-based localisation [【24†12】]. The RSS measurement is usually affected by noise, circumstance, multipath, and so on. The application is limited for its low accuracy of location [【25†5】, 【26†13】].\nL156: The TDOA geolocation system is often called hyperbolic location, locating an emitter with TDOA measurements between three or more stations. The accuracy of TDOA measurements depends mainly on the bandwidth of the signals [【27†14】]. The FH signal usually occupies a wide band, hence high accuracy can be obtained in TDOA estimation, which leads to accurate location estimate.\nL157: In the conventional TDOA geolocation systems, there are two steps to get the location estimate, first to measure the TDOA between different sensors, and then use the TDOA measurements to solve the TDOA geolocation equations. TDOA estimation and TDOA geolocation have been extensively studied in the past decades.\nL158: For TDOA estimation of FH signals, Carter proposed a generalised correlation method in [【28†15】], Stein derived the maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) and the Cramer–Rao lower bound (CRB) for the differential delay and Doppler in the case of unknown and deterministic signals [【29†16】]. Sadler considered the case of time-delay estimation bounds for FH signals in frequency-selective fading channels [【19†2】]. Wang proposed a linear method based on phase difference for TDOA estimation of FH signals [【30†17】].\nL159: This method is computationally efficient, but the accuracy depends on the estimation accuracy of the carriers difference, and the phase ambiguity still needs to be solved. Goetz showed the period peaks in the correlation of FH signals [【31†18】], but has not given a solution based on that.\nL160: For TDOA localisation, Torrieri proposed a least squares estimator (LSE) by linearising the non-linear equations with Taylor series expanding [【22†6】], Chan and Ho gives a two-step weighted least squares algorithm, where an intermediate variable is used to linearised the non-linear relations [【20†3】].\nL161: It has been demonstrated that the conventional two-step method are suboptimal, since the TDOA measurements do not follow the constraint that all measurements should be consistent for a location of a single emitter. Moreover, most of the conventional algorithms focused on the case of a single emitter. When there are multiple emitters, the measurements association problem has not been well addressed.\nL162: To overcome the shortcoming of the conventional two-step method, direct localisation methods have been proposed in [【32†19】-【33†25】]. These methods do not need to estimate the intermediate emitter location dependent variables, but estimate the location directly. Weiss proposed the direct position determination (DPD) concept first in 2004, and derived the DPD estimator for the cases of a single emitter and multiple emitters in [【32†19】-【34†23】].\nL163: The DPD approaches make use of the cross correlation function between sensors to construct an objective function, and then search the peaks in a defined two-dimension or three-dimension grid to get the location estimate. Weiss focused mainly on the DPD research of antenna array based location system, which is more complicated than TDOA geolocation system.\nL164: For TDOA geolocation system, Naresh and Kay proposed the TDOA-based direct location estimator for the case of a single band-limited signal [【35†24】, 【33†25】]. They pointed out that sampling the signal in time leads to a quantisation-like effect on the emitter position estimation. However, the problem can be avoided via using the signal model in frequency domain, because the TDOA estimate can be searched with any interval.\nL165: The above direct location methods do all processing at the centre node, that is not feasible when the capabilities of computation and communication are limited. In the case of that, Fowler proposed distributed computation and data compression method for DPD algorithms [【36†26】].\nL166: In this paper, we consider the case of multiple FH stationary emitters and a network of stationary sensors for TDOA geolocation. The two-dimensional case is assumed for simplicity, and extension to the three-dimensional case is straightforward. Using the received signal model in frequency domain, we derived the direct LSE of emitter location, for the case of unknown signals’ waveforms, which appears commonly in non-cooperative communications.\nL167: Under the assumption of Gaussian noise, the LSE is equal to the MLE.\nL168: The paper is structured as follows. Section 2 introduces the received signal model and some assumptions. Section 3 gives the derivation of CRB. In Section 4, we first give the direct TDOA geolocation algorithm for a single emitter, and then the resolution of location is analysed. Based on them, an extension of the new method for multiple FH emitters are given at last. In Section 5, numerical simulation results are provided to demonstrate the performance of the proposed method.\nL169: We show that the proposed method outperforms the conventional two-step method and it is effective in locating multiple FH emitters. Conclusions are provided in Section 6.\nL170: ## 2 Problem statement\nL171: \nL172: Considering that the FH signals are transmitted from Q static emitters, then they are received at L separated receivers in the presence of noise. The l th receiver's location is Z_{l}, and the q th emitter's location is P_{q}. Then the complex envelope of the L receivers’ outputs for flat-fading channels can be modelled as\nL173: \nL174: 【37†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0001】(1)\nL175: where 【38†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0002】 is the baseband signal of the h th hop in the q th FH signal, and 【39†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0003】 is the transmission time delay between the q th transmitter and the l th receiver. Since the TDOA information is used for localisation, we assume that 【40†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0004】, hence\nL176: 【41†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0005】(2)\nL177: \nL178: β_{l, q} accounts for the corresponding channel attenuation factor and the signal path phase-shift. T_{d} is the duration time of each hop, H is the number of hops, 【42†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0006】 are white Gaussian noise which are uncorrelated with 【43†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0007】 and uncorrelated with each other.\nL179: \nL180: We assume that:\nL181:   1. The signals are realisations of zero-mean, wide-sense stationary, complex Gaussian random processes, and uncorrelated with each other.\nL182:   2. The observation duration T is long enough, within which the signal can reach both the nearest and the farthest receivers.\nL183: In frequency domain, the complex envelope received by each station can be represented by its Fourier coefficients defined by\nL184: \nL185: 【44†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0008】(3)\nL186: where f_{m}  = m /T, m  = 0, 1, 2, …, is the m th coefficient and frequency. Then the Fourier coefficients of 【45†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0009】 and 【46†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0010】 are denoted by 【47†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0011】 and 【48†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0012】, respectively.\nL187: Since the FH signals occupy only a part of the total hop band W, we denote M as the number of Fourier coefficients. Consider a single emitter whose coordinates is denoted by P, we defined the following vectors:\nL188: 【49†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0013】(4)\nL189: \nL190: Next, define\nL191: \nL192: 【50†Im