25】 is the CRB of TDOA estimate, which has been derived in [【53†27】], and\nL223: \nL224: 【64†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0026】(13)\nL225: \nL226: Where B is the bandwidth of a single hop, W is the total hop band, and SNR is the signal to noise ratio. Let 【65†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0027】, then we have\nL227: \nL228: 【66†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0028】(14)\nL229: \nL230: where\nL231: 【67†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0029】(15)\nL232: \nL233: 【68†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0030】(16)\nL234: 【69†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0031】 is the distance between the emitter and the l th receiver, c is the velocity of light. When the errors of estimate between different parameters are uncorrelated, 【70†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0032】 is diagonal. Then 【71†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0033】. Substituting (【72†15】) and (【73†16】) into (【74†14】), gives\nL235: 【75†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0034】(17)\nL236: \nL237: where 【76†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0035】 is unrelated to the emitter position, and\nL238: \nL239: 【77†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0036】(18)\nL240: It can be found that (【78†18】) is the same to the result obtained in [【25†5】], and it coincides with the conclusion that the two-step method is equivalent to the direct location asymptotically in [【79†22】]. If 【80†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0037】 for all l, then we have\nL241: \nL242: 【81†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0038】(19)\nL243: ## 4 Location algorithm\nL244: ### 4.1 Single emitter location algorithm\nL245: \nL246: In this section, we give the direct TDOA geolocation algorithm to solve the location problem of the FH signals. Since the noise is white, Gaussian, and independent, identically distributed, the MLE coincides with the LSE. The single emitter MLE of 【82†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0039】 can be given by\nL247: \nL248: 【83†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0040】(20)\nL249: \nL250: where\nL251: 【84†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0041】(21)\nL252: \nL253: With respect to 【85†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0042】, and minimising above leads to\nL254: \nL255: 【86†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0043】(22)\nL256: \nL257: Substituting (【87†22】) into (【88†21】), and simplifying, gives\nL258: \nL259: 【89†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0044】(23)\nL260: \nL261: Therefore,\nL262: \nL263: 【90†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0045】\nL264: 【91†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0046】(24)\nL265: \nL266: Denote\nL267: \nL268: 【92†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0047】(25)\nL269: \nL270: Define the matrix 【93†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0048】\nL271: \nL272: 【94†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0049】(26)\nL273: \nL274: Then we have\nL275: \nL276: 【95†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0050】(27)\nL277: \nL278: Putting (【96†27】) back into (【97†24】), we have\nL279: 【98†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0051】(28)\nL280: \nL281: Let\nL282: \nL283: 【99†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0052】(29)\nL284: \nL285: Then, (【100†28】) can be represented as\nL286: \nL287: 【101†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0053】(30)\nL288: \nL289: Hence, the emitter position estimate will be\nL290: \nL291: 【102†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0054】(31)\nL292: ### 4.2 Resolution of location analysis\nL293: The estimation resolution of TDOA is limited by the peak lobe width of correlation, and the peak lobe width of correlation is limited by the bandwidth of signals [【27†14】]. When there is another TDOA in the peak lobe, it is hard to identify it, hence the peak lobe width determines the resolution of TDOA estimate.\nL294: If we treat the correlation function as a one-dimensional spectrum, then we can treat the objective function of direct localisation as a two-dimensional spectrum, and the range of the peak lobe will determine the resolution of localisation. We noted that the range of confidence ellipse depends on the estimation accuracy of TDOA: CRB_{τ}, and the geometric relation between positions of emitter and receivers: G. Then the accuracy of localisation can be limited by the range of the ellipse.\nL295: Then we can apply the result to the shape of peak lobe of the two-dimensional spectrum, it is also an ellipse, which depends on the peak lobe width of correlation and the relations between positions of emitter and receivers. Then the semi-major (i) and semi-minor (ii) and orientation (θ) of the ellipse can be defined as follows:\nL296: 【103†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0055】(32)\nL297: \nL298: where 【104†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0056】, and C_{i, j} is the entry at the i th row and the j th column. When there is another FH emitter in the ellipse, it is hard to identify it, hence the range of the ellipse determines the resolution of location estimate.\nL299: ### 4.3 Method for multiple FH emitters direct TDOA geolocation\nL300: \nL301: When there are multiple FH emitters which can be identified by the resolution, the process of direct TDOA geolocation will contain multiple peaks search. The effect of peaks produced by other emitters can be eliminated by the resolution analysis. Suppose the number of emitters is Q, then the steps of method for multiple FH emitters can be described as follows:\nL302:   1. Define a grid containing all the FH emitters positions, calculate 【105†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0057】 for each grid point, i  = 1.\nL303:   2. Search the i th peak of 【106†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0058】, obtain the i th emitter position estimate 【107†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0059】.\nL304:   3. Calculate the range of the i th ellipse by (【108†32】) with 【109†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0060】, and set the value of points in the ellipse to zero.\nL305:   4. If i  = Q, break; else i  = i  + 1, return to ii.\nL306: ## 5 Numerical simulation\nL307: To examine the performance of the proposed direct TDOA geolocation method, denoted by DG, Monte Carlo simulations are performed and the results are compared with the conventional two-step method and the CRB.\nL308: Considering four sensors located at coordinates 【110†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0061】, 【111†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0062】, 【112†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0063】, 【113†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0064】, and the emitter located at 【114†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0065】.\nL309: The hop band is 10 MHz, the hop rate is 10,000 hop/s, the number of hops is 10, the modulation is BPSK and the data rate is 20 kbps. The searched grid is 2 km × 2 km region around the true emitter location, the step of search is 10 m. The conventional two-step method denoted by LSE is that, first TDOAs are estimated by generalised correlation, and then the LSE method is used to estimate the emitter location. The signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is varied from 0 to 20 dB.\nL310: For each SNR, we performed 200 Monte Carlo experiments.\nL311: Fig. 【115†1】 shows the comparison of the standard deviation of the DG and the LSE against the CRB for the emitter at 【116†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0066】. It can be seen that the performance of the DG is better than that of the LSE, especially at low SNR. At high SNR, the performance of DG approach to the CRB asymptotically, and the performance difference between the DG and the LSE become smaller.\nL312: Fig. 【117†2】 shows the spectrum of direct TDOA geolocation for one realisation at SNR of 20 dB. Note that the shape of the peak range around the emitter location can be described by the dashed black ellipse obtained by (【108†32】). Next, we added another FH emitter, located at 【118†Image: urn:x-wiley:17519675:media:sil2bf00430:sil2bf00430-math-0067】. For each SNR, we also performed 200 Monte Carlo experiments. Fig. 【119†3】 shows the 200 direct TDOA geolocation results of the two emitters at SNR of 10 dB.\nL313: It demonstrated that the effect of peaks produced by other emitters can be eliminated by the proposed method. Fig. 【120†4】 shows the comparison of the average standard deviation of the two emitters estimation denoted by DG-AVG against the CRB for different SNR values. The performances of the two FH emitters denoted by DG-1 and DG-2 are also compared. It indicated that, the performances of the two FH emitters are comparable with each other.\nL314: With respect to Fig. 【115†1】, the performances of the two FH emitters deviate farer from the CRB than the case of a single FH emitter, due to the small separation between them. Though, the CRB can still predict the performances of the two FH emitters approximately. The results proved that the method for multiple FH emitters direct TDOA geolocation is efficient.\nL315: Fig. 1\n"
